<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Protestantism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Protestantism is the branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> that arose from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> of the sixteenth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> and others broke with the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. Its main traditions, Lutheran, Reformed and Anglican, rejected papal authority and stressed the authority of scripture and salvation by faith. It became the established religion of much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and spread worldwide through migration and missions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/protestantism.jpg" alt="Protestantism" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torsten Schleese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lutherbibel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Protestantism follows the theology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>, a movement that began in the 16th century with the aim of reforming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> of perceived errors and abuses. It began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1517, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> published his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a> in reaction to abuses in the sale of indulgences. Luther argued against buying or earning forgiveness and claimed instead that salvation is a gift God gives to those who have faith.</p><p>The name comes from a protest. Six princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the rulers of fourteen Imperial Free Cities issued a protest against the edict of the Diet of Speyer in 1529 and were the first to be called Protestants. Lutheranism spread into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, and Calvinist churches spread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. The separation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglicanism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Protestants hold to justification by faith alone, the priesthood of all believers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> as the highest authority for faith and practice. They reject papal supremacy, and early Protestants rejected transubstantiation. Lutherans affirmed a corporeal presence of Christ in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Holy Communion</a>, along with Catholics, while the Reformed affirmed a real spiritual presence.</p><p>The movement soon divided into branches: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglicanism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> or Reformed Christianity, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodism</a>, which grew from a revival within the 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wesley">John Wesley</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a>, who hold that baptism should be given only to professing believers. The Radical Reformation, which had no state sponsorship and faced intense persecution from both Catholics and mainstream Protestants, gave rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptism</a>, whose descendants include the Amish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> affected marriage and family, education, the political and social order, the economy and the arts. Protestant churches reject a celibate priesthood and allow their clergy to marry. Freedom of conscience gained priority after Luther refused to recant at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in 1521, and Rhode Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> combined democratic constitutions with freedom of religion. Protestants founded hospitals, schools and aid organisations, and in the 19th century many took part in the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, prison reform and woman suffrage. Estimates of the number of Protestants worldwide range from 600 million to 1 billion, because scholars disagree on which denominations count.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Protestantism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zwingli’s symbolic understanding of the Eucharist, which he defended against Martin Luther at the Marburg Colloquy of 1529, became a defining feature of Reformed <b>Protestantism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Lorraine remained firmly Catholic: Charles III founded the Jesuit University of Pont-à-Mousson in 1572, and the dukes repressed <b>Protestantism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>As <b>Protestantism</b> spread among the laity, dissent grew against the Catholic rule of the Teutonic Knights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto Henry introduced Lutheranism in 1556, and Frederick III turned to Reformed <b>Protestantism</b>, ordering images removed from churches and imposing strict church discipline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Protestantism</b> spread widely in the sixteenth century, but after 1620 the dynasty enforced Catholicism in Bohemia and Austria, confiscated rebel estates and drove tens of thousands of Protestants into exile, while the Jesuits took over schools and universities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Protestant Christianity</b>, spread by the London Missionary Society, became the established religion of the court after 1869.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
